EXT. SWAMP FERRY - NIGHT

Black water. Dead trees. A flat barge poled through reeds. One lantern.

MORVAN, 70s, ferryman, bent, poles in silence. HESPER, 20s, a cloaked wanderer, sits cross-legged at the prow. BRIAR, 12, wild-haired, trails a hand in the water. SABLE, 30s, a hooded archer, keeps watch at the stern.

BRIAR
It's cold.

MORVAN
Pull your hand in.

BRIAR
It's not that cold.

MORVAN
Pull it in.

Briar sulks. Sable says nothing. Hesper does not look up.

A bubble rises beside the barge. Then another.

THE DROWNED KING (O.S.)
(from beneath the water, hollow)
Ferryman. You've brought me guests.

Morvan stops poling. His knuckles whiten.

MORVAN
No guests, Majesty. Just cargo.

THE DROWNED KING (O.S.)
Cargo has hands. I count eight.

Briar yanks her arm out. Water streams from it, black and thick. Sable draws an arrow. Hesper lifts her gaze.

HESPER
Poles, Morvan. Faster.

SABLE
Don't.

THE DROWNED KING (O.S.)
You may go. The child stays.

Briar clings to Hesper's cloak. Sable nocks the arrow. Morvan breathes through his teeth.

MORVAN
(low)
I never agreed to this.

HESPER
Neither did she.

The barge shudders. A pale hand grips the gunwale beside Briar. Then another. Sable looses the arrow. It sinks without a splash.
